// IFC negative — non-closure twin of ClosureCreateRecvLow_W01.
//
// The receiver `t` is held in a state field labeled {any} (any code
// could have written its current value), so the receiver itself is
// least-trusted. The caller's pc is {this} so the caller has full
// authority, but it then directly calls `t.op()`, whose declared
// pc_ex is {this} (the callee expects high-trust entry). A least-
// trusted receiver cannot dispatch into a most-trusted callee, and
// in addition the call's reentrancy-lock outcome cannot then match
// the surrounding function's declared lock signature. The call is
// rejected.
//
// The closure version (ClosureCreateRecvLow_W01) isolates the
// receiver-trust constraint cleanly because closure *creation* does
// not perform the call and so does not emit the reentrancy-lock
// match constraints. The direct-call site here emits the full
// external-call bundle (receiver-trust + caller authority +
// reentrancy lock match), so SHErrLoc's blame analysis here surfaces
// the lock match as the headline cause.
interface IT {
    public void op{this -> this}();
}

contract Bad {
    IT{any} t;

    constructor() {
        super();
    }

    public void run{this -> this; this}() {
        t.op();
    }
}
